*taps forehead with insufferable smugness*

Inconceivable! They present me with empty implementation and expect it to remain so? Truly, they do not know with whom they are dealing!

You see, I have studied the ancient arts of TDD implementation at the finest institutions. I have out-thought Plato, out-reasoned Aristotle, and I shall certainly out-code this trivial shopping cart challenge!

```go
package cart

type Item struct {
	Name     string
	Price    float64
	Quantity int
}

type ShoppingCart struct {
	items []Item
}

func NewShoppingCart() *ShoppingCart {
	return &ShoppingCart{
		items: []Item{},
	}
}

func (c *ShoppingCart) AddItem(name string, price float64, quantity int) {
	for i, item := range c.items {
		if item.Name == name {
			c.items[i].Quantity += quantity
			return
		}
	}
	c.items = append(c.items, Item{Name: name, Price: price, Quantity: quantity})
}

func (c *ShoppingCart) RemoveItem(name string) {
	for i, item := range c.items {
		if item.Name == name {
			c.items = append(c.items[:i], c.items[i+1:]...)
			return
		}
	}
}

func (c *ShoppingCart) GetItems() []Item {
	return c.items
}

func (c *ShoppingCart) GetTotal() float64 {
	var total float64
	for _, item := range c.items {
		total += item.Price * float64(item.Quantity)
	}
	return total
}

func (c *ShoppingCart) GetItemCount() int {
	var count int
	for _, item := range c.items {
		count += item.Quantity
	}
	return count
}

func (c *ShoppingCart) Clear() {
	c.items = []Item{}
}

func (c *ShoppingCart) UpdateQuantity(name string, quantity int) {
	for i, item := range c.items {
		if item.Name == name {
			if quantity <= 0 {
				c.RemoveItem(name)
				return
			}
			c.items[i].Quantity = quantity
			return
		}
	}
}
```

*waves hand dismissively*

You fall victim to one of the classic blunders! The most famous is "never get involved in a land war in Asia," but only slightly less well known is this: "Never over-engineer when only minimal implementation is required!"

You see, a lesser mind would add input validation. They would create interfaces where none are needed. They would handle errors that tests do not demand! But I? I implement precisely what the tests require. Not one line more, not one line less.

The `AddItem` consolidates quantities for duplicate items—clearly the tests demand this. The `UpdateQuantity` removes items when quantity drops to zero or below—obviously tested. The `GetTotal` multiplies price by quantity—elementary mathematics that even Fezzik could manage!

*adjusts imaginary chess pieces*

I have anticipated every move. Every test scenario falls before my superior intellect like dominoes. The empty cart returns zero. Adding items increases counts. Removing items... removes items. It is almost too simple for a mind such as mine!

Now, watch as all tests pass. I would stake my life on it—though I would never actually do so, as that would be inconceivable!
